#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228851 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6af40e66-155c-57b8-b427-95259a2c1034" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "90e0e1d2-ca8a-5919-9891-599af549f2d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21618" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} + 3 y = 2\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  A e ^ {-3x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\cos{x}.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos x + \\beta  \\sin x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =   - \\alpha \\sin {x} +  \\beta \\cos {x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n( - \\alpha \\sin {x} +  \\beta \\cos {x}) + 3(\\alpha \\cos x + \\beta  \\sin x) = 2\\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(3\\alpha+\\beta)\\cos x + (-\\alpha + 3\\beta) \\sin x = 2\\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac 3 5,$ $\\beta = \\dfrac 1 5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac{3}{5} \\cos(x) + \\dfrac{1}{5} \\sin(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228852 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7a2bbc-4172-591e-98eb-f7586d3ae06b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "5769fd27-5fa4-5c85-b927-c87bedca3ea3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21619" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 4 y = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2 \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 4 y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(3x) + \\beta  \\sin(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =   - 3\\alpha \\sin(3x) +  3\\beta \\cos(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2 ( - 3\\alpha \\sin(3x) +  3\\beta \\cos(3x)) + 4(\\alpha \\cos(3x)+ \\beta  \\sin(3x)) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta + 4\\alpha)\\cos(3x) + (-6\\alpha + 4 \\beta) \\sin(3x) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta +4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha +4\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=\\dfrac{11}{13}, \\beta=\\dfrac{10}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ny_p(x) =  \\dfrac{11}{13}\\cos(3x) + \\dfrac{10}{13} \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  A e^{-2x} + \\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{11}{13} }\\right)\\cos(3x) \n+ \n\\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{10}{13} }\\right) \\sin(3x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228853 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05d3144f-53da-5608-a07b-d1d5d672bf0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "3b584b5f-8398-5f18-923d-47a3fb09adca" :question/math-academy-id "e-21620" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' + 3y = 26 \\sin(2x), \\qquad y(0) =  1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 3y = 26 \\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 3y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $26 \\sin(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(2x) + \\beta  \\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' =   - 2\\alpha \\sin(2x) +  2\\beta \\cos(2x)\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n( - 2\\alpha \\sin(2x) +  2\\beta \\cos(2x)) + 3 (\\alpha \\cos(2x)+ \\beta  \\sin(2x)) = 26 \\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta+3\\alpha)\\cos(2x) + (-2\\alpha +3 \\beta) \\sin(2x) = 26 \\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta +3\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha +3\\beta =26\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-4, \\beta=6.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =  - 4\\cos(2x) + 6\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{-3x} - 4\\cos(2x) + 6\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =1 \\\\ A-4+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 5e^{-3x} - 4\\cos(2x) + 6\\sin(2x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6af40e66-155c-57b8-b427-95259a2c1034" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbc17935-2db8-511e-a96b-f886bfba5a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{17}\\cos(x)+\\frac{32}{17}\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbc17935-2db8-511e-a96b-f886bfba5a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{17}\\cos(x)+\\frac{32}{17}\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379212} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7db62d-20b3-5a50-9d66-6354ba7e63ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5abf4eb-a16d-59fe-ae4c-da3f3734dace" :question/math-academy-id "q-332622" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(4\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +4\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{8}{17},\\beta =\\frac{32}{17}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{8}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{32}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6af40e66-155c-57b8-b427-95259a2c1034" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7212005c-1986-515b-851e-c89d6a1fcb6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379213} {:answer/id #uuid "7d28e55d-3afa-5dae-a859-56ddd75ff45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}\\cos(x)+\\frac{7}{5}\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d28e55d-3afa-5dae-a859-56ddd75ff45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}\\cos(x)+\\frac{7}{5}\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379214} :answer-field/id #uuid "546486d7-7082-5964-b77e-10ded3e7cd82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a0ec141-3c3b-575d-86b4-beb50eaf963b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332623" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +\\beta =7\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{14}{5},\\beta =\\frac{7}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{14}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6af40e66-155c-57b8-b427-95259a2c1034" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aef0022-2654-5a25-bb46-6ae9362d7b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}\\cos(x)+\\frac{4}{5}\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319050} {:answer/id #uuid "1361dbbd-6eaf-5807-a676-69facfd923b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aef0022-2654-5a25-bb46-6ae9362d7b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}\\cos(x)+\\frac{4}{5}\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319050} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9566028-7471-501e-bd7f-492ba6563068" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2145790b-68f6-55bf-951c-b255f46040c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332676" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(3\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{12}{5},\\beta =\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{12}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6af40e66-155c-57b8-b427-95259a2c1034" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee80fd5c-1152-52d7-9040-18d54ad7e497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319056} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d4c1e8-6e35-55ec-9561-28c79fb83134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(x)+\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee80fd5c-1152-52d7-9040-18d54ad7e497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319056} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc2444ce-4d51-5445-ae10-b762660dbb17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e284231-5833-58cc-bb3d-307106a8a4f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332754" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(4\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +4\\beta =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $\\beta =3-4\\alpha .$ Substituting into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n-\\alpha +4(3-4\\alpha )=-5\\,⇒\\,\\alpha =1.\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\beta =3-4\\alpha =3-4(1)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7a2bbc-4172-591e-98eb-f7586d3ae06b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9c1ef25-2730-55c2-9222-df1266a9f25a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9c1ef25-2730-55c2-9222-df1266a9f25a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379217} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbb5ec12-55f7-53ae-8257-ab51e91aaf61" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228082} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "436e7b10-7b16-5c7f-bc62-4023f424bf9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "436e7b10-7b16-5c7f-bc62-4023f424bf9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379216} :answer-field/id #uuid "db1c4c05-dad4-51e4-9b6c-8429b2468b4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7dd8dc08-2fc9-5908-9810-58211c6eb3df" :question/math-academy-id "q-332793" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-9y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{3x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta -9\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta -9\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -9\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{13},\\beta =-\\frac{6}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{6}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+(-\\frac{4}{13})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-\\frac{6}{13})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7a2bbc-4172-591e-98eb-f7586d3ae06b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "164ba21e-1d3d-5b6d-b668-cf9f28054098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379220} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7062ab-815e-5271-afda-43f6c16d869f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "164ba21e-1d3d-5b6d-b668-cf9f28054098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379220} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd9d25c-e771-5807-828e-3fef054d3388" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d27fe34-2810-51f0-b98f-3b67e08ddb0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d27fe34-2810-51f0-b98f-3b67e08ddb0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379223} :answer-field/id #uuid "b82b58df-876e-5e10-940d-c1c611e0970a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "476bd23f-79bf-50d4-8989-1188aefe9497" :question/math-academy-id "q-332836" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-8{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta -8\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\beta -8\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -8\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{15}{26},\\beta =-\\frac{5}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{15}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(-\\frac{15}{26})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-\\frac{5}{13})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7a2bbc-4172-591e-98eb-f7586d3ae06b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc517654-ce33-508c-bd29-da4121261bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379226} {:answer/id #uuid "85ea854b-d8b7-51af-83b0-064a065a2ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85ea854b-d8b7-51af-83b0-064a065a2ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379227} :answer-field/id #uuid "04f0462e-6e03-57c4-a62c-02f42e