}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))+16(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(16\\alpha +16\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(-16\\alpha +16\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha +16\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)) \\\\ -16\\alpha +16\\beta =9\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{9}{32},\\beta =\\frac{9}{32}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{9}{32}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\frac{9}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+(-\\frac{9}{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(\\frac{9}{32})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6af40e66-155c-57b8-b427-95259a2c1034" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f6e1235-d123-53b2-b171-737560dbf5dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{10}\\cos(x)+\\frac{19}{10}\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f6e1235-d123-53b2-b171-737560dbf5dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{10}\\cos(x)+\\frac{19}{10}\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379232} :answer-field/id #uuid "f523fe47-387d-59da-8547-a0ec9b08a934" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd21ba85-8f41-5ff6-aef4-4eb449f71fff" :question/math-academy-id "q-332870" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(\\beta -3\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\beta -3\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\alpha +3\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives: $\\alpha =-\\frac{7}{10},$ $\\beta =\\frac{19}{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{7}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{19}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7a2bbc-4172-591e-98eb-f7586d3ae06b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e30853a-1dab-532d-8e61-a49458bfc95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e30853a-1dab-532d-8e61-a49458bfc95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379235} :answer-field/id #uuid "53741043-aab3-54ef-85da-76b6fc1cde4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379234} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47d98d42-8268-5816-bea2-0689f5a62ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47d98d42-8268-5816-bea2-0689f5a62ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379237} :answer-field/id #uuid "e091bd4c-7b33-5b7d-9d30-e543e29224fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10d0f5cf-3c42-59ed-8eee-a85dd6e32991" :question/math-academy-id "q-332875" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-10y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-10{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))-10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(20\\beta -10\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(-20\\alpha -10\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}20\\beta -10\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)) \\\\ -20\\alpha -10\\beta =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{11}{50},\\beta =\\frac{13}{50}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{11}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\frac{13}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(\\frac{11}{50})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(\\frac{13}{50})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7a2bbc-4172-591e-98eb-f7586d3ae06b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c40c8eb-5a7d-5f77-a247-418a36d84580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340826} {:answer/id #uuid "16a9d721-3b90-5077-9935-9d399a8e802e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c40c8eb-5a7d-5f77-a247-418a36d84580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340826} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e474bbf-f0d8-549c-b22f-d0117f42fc52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "745fc73b-7d5d-5e5b-88fb-b7136fbc60ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{52}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "745fc73b-7d5d-5e5b-88fb-b7136fbc60ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{52}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340829} :answer-field/id #uuid "be4213cd-5a08-5eef-8d12-4bc2931bd8b4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "899be0a0-f9d5-5a3b-aa8a-2702864a79e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332886" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-8{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta -8\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\beta -8\\alpha =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -8\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{13},\\beta =-\\frac{19}{52}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{19}{52}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(\\frac{1}{13})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-\\frac{19}{52})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05d3144f-53da-5608-a07b-d1d5d672bf0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a59bc6f-042b-5cc9-b0d5-f06a99bdb6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^x-4\\cos(2x)-2\\sin(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a59bc6f-042b-5cc9-b0d5-f06a99bdb6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^x-4\\cos(2x)-2\\sin(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319089} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc9f64e3-3bfa-5ad5-84ce-6b616d0166fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ead02c21-80ee-5a57-89b6-6fd53261f316" :question/math-academy-id "q-332972" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta -\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-2\\alpha -\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta -\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha -\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-4,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{1\\cdot 0}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =3 \\\\ A-4-0 & =3 \\\\ A & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=7{e}^{x}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05d3144f-53da-5608-a07b-d1d5d672bf0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0c1d3be-7eb2-5e09-a28d-60113195142f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{-3x}-2\\cos(2x)+3\\sin(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319092} {:answer/id #uuid "74efbd9c-f941-5f1f-953b-df74032f38a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c1d3be-7eb2-5e09-a28d-60113195142f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{-3x}-2\\cos(2x)+3\\sin(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319092} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8042830-d5c6-52cb-ad78-6da9174efc8f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc1b6cd3-30a1-5248-b1ec-a4044de0f133" :question/math-academy-id "q-332973" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta +3\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-2\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta +3\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha +3\\beta =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =1 \\\\ A-2-0 & =1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{-3x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05d3144f-53da-5608-a07b-d1d5d672bf0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df6b8fa9-f18f-5f95-8880-57c3e3d9151f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-4x}+3\\cos(2x)-6\\sin(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379240} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0ebf7c-0c30-5c6f-929f-27a2a79ddddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df6b8fa9-f18f-5f95-8880-57c3e3d9151f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-4x}+3\\cos(2x)-6\\sin(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379240} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7480e52-6175-5fb1-a113-8410ee27569c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14e79ab9-95dd-579a-aae2-e3c97924b12a" :question/math-academy-id "q-332974" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta +4\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-2\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta +4\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha +4\\beta =-30\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\beta =-6.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-4\\cdot 0}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =1 \\\\ A+3-0 & =1 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{-4x}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05d3144f-53da-5608-a07b-d1d5d672bf0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9353d661-137d-5bc6-81dc-71