stants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta +3\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-2\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta +3\\alpha =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =5 \\\\ A+3+0 & =5 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-3x}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05d3144f-53da-5608-a07b-d1d5d672bf0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/ans